# Staging accumulator

This repo’s feature-PR base is **`staging`**, never the default branch
(`main`). Default-branch PRs are the periodic **promotion** of `staging`
itself. Agents read this rule rather than restating the split in a command.

## Accumulator identity

- **`staging`** is the long-lived accumulator. `/commit` on the local lane
  lands here. Feature PRs opened by `/push` from a non-default, non-`staging`
  head use `--base staging`.
- **Default branch is never a feature-PR base.** `/push` stops if an existing
  or pinned PR has `base=<default>` and `head != staging`.
- Promotion is `/push` run from `staging` (or pinned at that promotion PR):
  head `staging` → base default.

## Feature and Cloud branch identity

Human-created feature branches use **`sdd/RES-###`** (the Linear identifier
in the branch name). `/dispatch` does not create that branch, a worktree, or
any other execution environment; it emits confirmed command cards only.
Do not invent a `cursor/RES-*` intake identity — that pattern matches nothing
this repo has shipped.

**Cloud-agent heads:** observed `origin` shape is `cursor/<slug>-<4 hex>`
(never `cursor/RES-###`). A cloud PR is identified by that **head pattern**,
not by author and not by an `RES-###` in the branch name. `/intake` is the
inbound firewall for those PRs.

## Done-on-staging

A feature PR carries its own not-previously-merged commits with `/commit`’s
`Fixes RES-###` trailer, so Linear’s `On PR merge → Done` fires when that PR
merges into **`staging`**. Direct commits on `staging` (the local lane) still
need the promotion PR to carry the aggregated closing line — see
[.cursor/rules/linear-automation.mdc](.cursor/rules/linear-automation.mdc).

## No agent `gh pr merge`

Merging is the operator’s job in the GitHub UI. Commands (`/push`, `/intake`, `/commit`,
`/triage`, `/dispatch`, `/ready-merge-release`) forbid `gh pr merge`; the
git-stage guard blocks it in Shell. Never compensate a missed Done transition
with `save_issue`. Draft and ready PRs go through
`/ready-merge-release PR#`; only that command may mark a clean draft ready, and
the operator alone merges. CodeRabbit chat/Plan/Triage do not write Linear.

## Dispatch portfolio and daily queue

`/dispatch` gives every scoped Backlog issue one grounded route. Its
operator-approved `groom-portfolio` batch may set unambiguous
project/milestone/priority and a verified estimate across the full approved
Backlog scope, but every non-wave issue remains **Backlog with `cycle=null`**.
Estimate stays optional when effort evidence is absent.

Daily activation is a separate approval:

- Count every in-scope **Todo issue already in the live current cycle** before
  choosing any Backlog promotion.
- Preferred minimum is 5 total active and hard maximum is 10. Fill only
  `max(0, 10 - activeCount)` slots with the highest-ranked ready Backlog
  issues. A shortage below 5 is reported. An over-cap queue receives no new
  promotion and no automatic demotion.
- `activate-daily-wave` is the only dispatch scope allowed to move Backlog →
  Todo/current cycle, and only its exact approved IDs may move.
- Re-resolve the current cycle at apply time. Portfolio metadata failures and
  stale activation items are independently deferred.
- The post-apply re-read must confirm **Todo in the current cycle**, approved
  scheduling fields, and an executable `/design` or `/sdd-to-tdd` route before
  a card is emitted. Re-read every existing and proposed daily-queue issue;
  failed or partial promotions are excluded explicitly.

`/triage`'s Urgent+High WIP gate controls its filing floor and Urgent
fast-lane intake. It does not change dispatch's 5–10 total-active calculation
or make blocked work ready. Priority remains a ranking signal.

## Cloud recommendations (dashboard, never dispatch execution)

After post-apply confirmation, `/dispatch` may show optional Cloud
parallelization recommendations. Priority, security/P0 surface,
unit-decidability, acceptance-criterion coverage, and verified disjoint
write-sets are recommendation evidence only; they never control daily
activation. High-risk work may enter the queue and is marked
sequential/local-first.

The command never assigns/delegates an issue to the Cursor integration, writes
a spawn-triggering integration mention, creates a branch/worktree, or invokes
an agent. The operator decides whether to launch a recommendation. Cloud
Agents open their own `cursor/<slug>-<4 hex>` PRs; Dashboard → Cloud Agents →
Base Branch stays `staging`. Every returning Cloud PR passes through
`/intake` before `/push`.
